### What is multi-datacenter Kafka replication?

Kafka's built-in replication keeps copies within one cluster across brokers, but it does not span regions, because stretching the in-sync replica set across high-latency links would cripple write latency. So cross-region copying uses a separate tool. MirrorMaker 2, built on Kafka Connect, is the standard. It runs source and checkpoint connectors that read from the source cluster and produce into the target cluster.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

// Producer in Mumbai region writes to local cluster
public class MumbaiTransferProducer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "mumbai-kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", "SAV2001", "DEBIT 50000"));
            System.out.println("Rajesh's transfer written to Mumbai cluster");
            // MirrorMaker 2 will replicate this to Chennai as 'mumbai.transfers'
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Rajesh's transfer written to Mumbai cluster
```

### How does topic naming and loop prevention work?

MirrorMaker 2 by default prefixes mirrored topics with the source cluster alias. A topic `transfers` from the Mumbai cluster appears as `mumbai.transfers` in Chennai. This prevents infinite replication loops in active-active, because each cluster only forwards its own locally-originated topics, not the already-prefixed mirrored ones.

```bash
# MirrorMaker 2 configuration for Mumbai -> Chennai replication
# mm2.properties
clusters = mumbai, chennai
mumbai.bootstrap.servers = mumbai-kafka-1:9092
chennai.bootstrap.servers = chennai-kafka-1:9092
mumbai->chennai.enabled = true
mumbai->chennai.topics = transfers, settlements, account-events

# In Chennai, topics appear as:
# mumbai.transfers
# mumbai.settlements
# mumbai.account-events
```

```text
In Chennai cluster:
  mumbai.transfers      (mirrored from Mumbai)
  transfers             (local Chennai writes)
  chennai.transfers     (would appear in Mumbai if bidirectional)
```

### How does offset translation work?

Offsets are per-cluster, so offset 42 in Mumbai is not offset 42 in Chennai. MirrorMaker 2 writes checkpoint records that map source offsets to target offsets for each consumer group. The RemoteClusterUtils API lets a failed-over consumer translate its last committed Mumbai offset into the right Chennai offset.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.connect.mirror.RemoteClusterUtils;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.HashMap;

public class OffsetTranslationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Map<String, Object> config = new HashMap<>();
        config.put("bootstrap.servers", "chennai-kafka-1:9092");

        // Translate Mumbai offsets to Chennai offsets for the settlement group
        Map<TopicPartition, Long> translatedOffsets =
            RemoteClusterUtils.translateOffsets(
                config, "mumbai", "settlement-group", java.time.Duration.ofSeconds(10));

        translatedOffsets.forEach((tp, offset) ->
            System.out.println(tp + " -> Chennai offset: " + offset));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
mumbai.transfers-0 -> Chennai offset: 45023
mumbai.transfers-1 -> Chennai offset: 44890
```

### What is the active-passive design?

All producers and consumers run against the primary region. MirrorMaker 2 continuously copies topics and offsets to the standby. During a disaster you redirect clients to the standby, translate offsets, and resume.

| Aspect | Active-Passive |
|---|---|
| Writes | Primary region only |
| Standby | Warm, receives continuous replicated data |
| Failover | Deliberate operation with offset translation |
| Data loss window | Equal to replication lag |
| Conflict risk | None -- single writer |
| Capacity utilization | Standby mostly idle |

```bash
# Failover procedure for active-passive
# 1. Redirect producers to Chennai
# 2. Translate offsets for consumer groups
kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server chennai-kafka-1:9092 \
  --group settlement-service --reset-offsets \
  --to-offset 45023 --topic mumbai.transfers --execute
# 3. Start consumers against Chennai cluster
```

### What is the active-active design and what are the hard problems?

Both regions accept writes and mirror to each other. Local users get low latency because they write to their nearest cluster. The hard problems are three: loop prevention, offset translation, and conflicting writes.

For money movement, true multi-master writes to the same account are dangerous, so banks usually pin write ownership of each account to a home region, effectively making it active-passive per key while using both regions fully.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import java.util.Properties;

// Active-active with home-region ownership
public class HomeRegionProducer {
    private final KafkaProducer<String, String> producer;
    private final String homeRegion; // "mumbai" or "chennai"

    public HomeRegionProducer(KafkaProducer<String, String> producer, String homeRegion) {
        this.producer = producer;
        this.homeRegion = homeRegion;
    }

    public void sendTransfer(String accountId, String payload) {
        String accountRegion = getHomeRegion(accountId);
        if (!accountRegion.equals(homeRegion)) {
            System.out.println("SKIP: Account " + accountId
                + " belongs to " + accountRegion + ", not " + homeRegion);
            return; // Route to the correct region instead
        }
        producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", accountId, payload));
        System.out.println("Sent transfer for " + accountId + " in " + homeRegion);
    }

    private String getHomeRegion(String accountId) {
        // Rajesh (SAV2001) -> Mumbai, Priya (SAV3001) -> Chennai
        return accountId.startsWith("SAV2") ? "mumbai" : "chennai";
    }
}
```

### How is active-passive different from active-active?

| Aspect | Active-Passive | Active-Active |
|---|---|---|
| Write regions | One (primary) | Both |
| Latency | Higher for remote users | Low for local users |
| Capacity utilization | Standby idle | Full utilization |
| Conflicts | None | Must handle or prevent |
| Loop prevention | Not needed | Required (topic prefix) |
| Offset translation | On failover only | Continuous |
| Complexity | Simple | High |
| Best for banking | Default choice | With home-region ownership |

```bash
# Active-active bidirectional MirrorMaker 2 config
clusters = mumbai, chennai
mumbai->chennai.enabled = true
chennai->mumbai.enabled = true
mumbai->chennai.topics = transfers, settlements
chennai->mumbai.topics = transfers, settlements
# Loop prevention: mumbai.transfers in Chennai is NOT re-mirrored back
```

### What about monitoring and recovery point objectives?

Replication lag defines your recovery point objective (RPO). You monitor MirrorMaker 2 lag closely, and you decide the recovery time objective (RTO) by how fast you can redirect clients and translate offsets.

```bash
# Monitor MirrorMaker 2 replication lag
kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server chennai-kafka-1:9092 \
  --describe --group mm2-mirror-source-connector
```

```text
GROUP                          TOPIC       PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG
mm2-mirror-source-connector    transfers   0          45023           45100           77
mm2-mirror-source-connector    transfers   1          44890           44950           60
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

For money movement, true multi-master writes to the same account are dangerous, so banks use active-active for read scaling and locality but pin write ownership of each account to a home region. This is effectively active-passive per key while using both regions fully. You must test failover regularly or it will not work when regulators ask. Replication lag should be alerted on, and you should drill the failover quarterly.
